<h1>Lead-acid technology</h1>
<p>How a lead-acid battery is built and why it ages: plates, grids, electrolyte, formation and the failure modes that decide service life.</p>
<h2>How to use this cluster</h2>
<p>Start from the failure you are trying to prevent, not from the component name. Capacity loss points to sulfation, aging or a charging problem; water loss points to overcharge or a hot environment; grid corrosion points to alloy and service life. Each article below is written to connect a symptom to a design or maintenance cause, so the cluster works as a diagnostic path rather than a glossary.</p>
<h2>The three trade-offs that decide a lead-acid design</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>CCA versus cycle life.</strong> Thin plates expose more surface area and deliver high cold-cranking current, but they corrode faster. Thick plates trade cranking performance for deep-cycle endurance.</li>
<li><strong>Pb-Ca versus Pb-Sb.</strong> Calcium alloys reduce water loss and maintenance; antimony alloys tolerate deeper cycling and are more forgiving of overcharge. The choice is a maintenance strategy, not a quality ranking.</li>
<li><strong>VRLA versus flooded.</strong> Valve-regulated designs are maintenance-free and spill-resistant but less tolerant of heat and overcharge; flooded designs need watering but survive abuse better in stationary duty.</li>
</ul>
<h2>What this cluster does not cover</h2>
<p>Application sizing, group-size selection, wholesale pricing and test standards live in their own clusters. Use the application hub for duty-cycle fit, the data site for dimensions and CCA by group, and the testing site for the standards that govern a performance claim.</p>

